*Context: *Back in my senior year of undergrad (2015-2016), when I was
studying and interning in Beijing, I picked up a side gig to teach English
grammar at an after-school and weekend tutoring program. One of the
program=E2=80=99s teachers, who also had a daughter in the class, invited m=
e to
lunch before the cycle=E2=80=99s final class, where I politely consumed som=
e Baijiu
(Chinese liquor). I still recall, at the last class, one of the kids asking
me why my face was so red, as I have a pretty severe case of the Asian
flush.

On a Sunday afternoon, these cheeky seven and eight-year-old kids could
have been chasing soccer balls out in the sun, and, yet, here they were
learning about past participles from someone without any teaching
experience in English grammar. Driven by parents who hold tightly onto
their children=E2=80=99s education as a meritocratic ladder =E2=80=94 as sy=
mbolized by the
gaokao (college entrance exam) =E2=80=94 the private tutoring industry was =
in the
midst of explosive growth, producing unicorn companies like VIPKID and
Yuanfudao.

Then, in July 2021, the Chinese government banned for-profit private
tutoring. Concerned about the pressures on overworked students and the
escalating costs of education (with the assumption that limiting tutoring
costs would incentivize parents to have more babies), the government also
prohibited online platforms that allowed =E2=80=9Ccompanies to hire *tutors=
 from
outside of China who are native English speakers
<https://substack.com/redirect/765f182b-eb19-4185-a855-0346ada3fab5?j=3DeyJ=
1IjoiMmZseDYifQ.6O_iC1_GWCzChnbrHzSrs1hMpJpoODTUJm_FAk3tf6E>*.=E2=80=9D
The threat to the tutoring industry was existential, *reported
<https://substack.com/redirect/11ba180e-c9be-445c-9121-462a93c70990?j=3DeyJ=
1IjoiMmZseDYifQ.6O_iC1_GWCzChnbrHzSrs1hMpJpoODTUJm_FAk3tf6E>*
Yi-Ling Liu for *The New Yorker*:

*New Oriental let go of sixty thousand of its staff, the online
English-tutoring platform VIPKid shut down some of its services, and TAL
Education Group, renowned for its Math Olympiad-style courses, transitioned
to a =E2=80=9Cquality education=E2=80=9D program that teaches calligraphy i=
nstead of
calculus. One of China=E2=80=99s largest education companies, Yuanfudao, st=
arted
posting job listings for fashion designers, sparking rumors that it was
pivoting its business to winter jackets.*

In this week=E2=80=99s feature translation, we learn about how one platform=
, Zebra
English [=E6=96=91=E9=A9=AC], and its AI teacher Jessica have adapted. In a=
 longform
piece, *Renwu
*[=E4=BA=BA=E7=89=A9] gives us the perspective of the parents who sit by th=
eir kid=E2=80=99s side
(sometimes literally) as they take the English language journey. Backed by
Tencent in 2017, Zebra English is a subsidiary of Yuanfudao.

*Key Passages: A longtime flaw in China=E2=80=99s English learning environm=
ent is
the problem of =E2=80=9CMute English [=E5=93=91=E5=B7=B4=E8=8B=B1=E8=AF=AD]=
.=E2=80=9D*

   -

   *Parents don=E2=80=99t want their kids to learn Mute English like they o=
nce did.
   *Based in Nanchang, Jiangxi Province, Lin Lan is the mother of a
   second-grade daughter. Lin graduated from a prestigious Chinese universi=
ty
   known for foreign language studies. She always passed the English langua=
ge
   tests with flying colors (=E2=80=9Ca shining star in the exam-oriented e=
ducation
   system=E2=80=9D).
   -

   Yet, when it came to speaking English, she froze. She tells *Renwu*: =E2=
=80=9C*Once,
   walking on the streets of Beijing, two foreigners asked her for directio=
ns.
   She waved her hands frantically, like someone who couldn=E2=80=99t under=
stand
   English, and hid behind her husband. Her husband=E2=80=99s English was b=
arely at
   the CET-4 level, but he managed to communicate with them using broken,
   accented =E2=80=98survival English,=E2=80=99 gesturing until the foreign=
ers understood*.=E2=80=9D

*Another issue is the ruthless competition over English teachers. *For over
half a year, Xu Ming set her alarm to wake up at 3AM, in order to open up
an app and book English classes for her child. She had to scramble to
reserve Anna, one of the top Filipino English teachers=C2=B9 on the platfor=
m.

   -

   Over time, Anna got assigned more administrative work and taught fewer
   classes, until she eventually disappeared from Xu Ming=E2=80=99s booking=
 list. Xu
   Ming felt helpless: =E2=80=9C*On the other hand, any uncertain news from=
 the
   Philippines also weighs heavily on her mind. For example, news reports o=
f
   typhoons or earthquakes in the Philippines would cause her anxiety,
   worrying about her teacher=E2=80=99s safety and the disruption to teachi=
ng. She
   constantly worries that this delicate transnational educational cable mi=
ght
   suddenly snap due to some distant disturbance*.=E2=80=9D
   -

   Zhao Li also felt powerless when she tried to find an in-person English
   teacher for her son. She had finally negotiated with an English teacher
   working at a local school in southern Hubei, but the teacher did not com=
e
   to the agreed-upon trial lesson and said that they were going to Shangha=
i
   to teach because the pay was higher there.

*A generation that desperately wants their children to learn English better
than they did meets a new technology that promises a solution to these
problems. *In early 2023, the Zebra English team decided to develop an
AI-powered tutor for spoken English.

   -

   To train AI teacher =E2=80=9CJessica=E2=80=9D, they cultivated a warm pe=
rsonality. The
   research team would conduct feedback sessions, in which they played =E2=
=80=9C*the
   role of students, deliberately giving incorrect, tricky, or even whimsic=
al
   answers to observe and correct Jessica=E2=80=99s reactions*.=E2=80=9D Th=
ey also reviewed
   25-minute trial lessons with children, analyzing Jessica=E2=80=99s respo=
nses for
   each situation.

