px 0;color:rgb(54,55,55);line-height:26px;font-size:16px"><strong>Context: =
</strong><span>Back in my senior year of undergrad (2015-2016), when I was =
studying and interning in Beijing, I picked up a side gig to teach English =
grammar at an after-school and weekend tutoring program. One of the program=
=E2=80=99s teachers, who also had a daughter in the class, invited me to lu=
nch before the cycle=E2=80=99s final class, where I politely consumed some =
Baijiu (Chinese liquor). I still recall, at the last class, one of the kids=
 asking me why my face was so red, as I have a pretty severe case of the As=
ian flush. </span></p><p style=3D"margin:0 0 20px 0;color:rgb(54,55,55);lin=
e-height:26px;font-size:16px">On a Sunday afternoon, these cheeky seven and=
 eight-year-old kids could have been chasing soccer balls out in the sun, a=
nd, yet, here they were learning about past participles from someone withou=
t any teaching experience in English grammar. Driven by parents who hold ti=
ghtly onto their children=E2=80=99s education as a meritocratic ladder =E2=
=80=94 as symbolized by the gaokao (college entrance exam) =E2=80=94 the pr=
ivate tutoring industry was in the midst of explosive growth, producing uni=
corn companies like VIPKID and Yuanfudao.</p><p style=3D"margin:0 0 20px 0;=
color:rgb(54,55,55);line-height:26px;font-size:16px"><span>Then, in July 20=
21, the Chinese government banned for-profit private tutoring. Concerned ab=
out the pressures on overworked students and the escalating costs of educat=
ion (with the assumption that limiting tutoring costs would incentivize par=
ents to have more babies), the government also prohibited online platforms =
that allowed =E2=80=9Ccompanies to hire </span><strong><a href=3D"https://s=
ubstack.com/redirect/765f182b-eb19-4185-a855-0346ada3fab5?j=3DeyJ1IjoiMmZse=
DYifQ.6O_iC1_GWCzChnbrHzSrs1hMpJpoODTUJm_FAk3tf6E" rel=3D"" style=3D"color:=
rgb(54,55,55);text-decoration:underline" target=3D"_blank">tutors from outs=
ide of China who are native English speakers</a></strong><span>.=E2=80=9D T=
he threat to the tutoring industry was existential, </span><strong><a href=
=3D"https://substack.com/redirect/11ba180e-c9be-445c-9121-462a93c70990?j=3D=
eyJ1IjoiMmZseDYifQ.6O_iC1_GWCzChnbrHzSrs1hMpJpoODTUJm_FAk3tf6E" rel=3D"" st=
yle=3D"color:rgb(54,55,55);text-decoration:underline" target=3D"_blank">rep=
orted</a></strong><span> Yi-Ling Liu for </span><em>The New Yorker</em><spa=
n>: </span></p><blockquote style=3D"border-left:4px solid #4dd0e1;margin:20=
px 0;padding:0"><p style=3D"margin:0 0 20px 0;color:rgb(54,55,55);margin-le=
ft:20px;line-height:26px;font-size:16px"><em>New Oriental let go of sixty t=
housand of its staff, the online English-tutoring platform VIPKid shut down=
 some of its services, and TAL Education Group, renowned for its Math Olymp=
iad-style courses, transitioned to a =E2=80=9Cquality education=E2=80=9D pr=
ogram that teaches calligraphy instead of calculus. One of China=E2=80=99s =
largest education companies, Yuanfudao, started posting job listings for fa=
shion designers, sparking rumors that it was pivoting its business to winte=
r jackets.</em></p></blockquote><p style=3D"margin:0 0 20px 0;color:rgb(54,=
55,55);line-height:26px;font-size:16px"><span>In this week=E2=80=99s featur=
e translation, we learn about how one platform, Zebra English [=E6=96=91=E9=
